# __NAME__

This is a cookie-authenticated KetJS API application. The generated `auth` module owns its account,
role, grant and assignment models. It does not depend on KetSuite or require a company model.

## Start and create an account

```bash
# Run from: __NAME__
npm install
npm run user:add
npm run dev
```

`user:add` prompts for a login and a hidden password of at least 15 characters. Provisioning runs
locally; it is absent from HTTP and agent surfaces. There is no default password or public signup.
For automation, feed a JSON object with `login` and `password` to the command's stdin. Do not put
credentials in command arguments or commit credential files.

The server listens on `http://127.0.0.1:3000`. The initial role grants `auth.me` and the sample note
operations. New operations and revoked grants are not automatically enabled for existing roles.

## Call the API

Enter a JSON object with `login` and `password` matching your provisioned account on curl's standard
input, then press Ctrl-D:

```bash
# Run from: __NAME__
mkdir -p .ket
curl --cookie-jar .ket/cookies --json @- http://127.0.0.1:3000/auth/login
```

```bash
# Run from: __NAME__
curl --cookie .ket/cookies http://127.0.0.1:3000/auth/me
curl --cookie .ket/cookies --header 'Origin: http://127.0.0.1:3000' --json '{"title":"My first note"}' http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/v1/notes/create
curl --cookie .ket/cookies http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/v1/notes
curl --cookie .ket/cookies --request POST http://127.0.0.1:3000/auth/logout
```

Notes belong to the signed-in account. The API ignores client-supplied ownership: creation stamps
the actor and reads filter by that actor. Anonymous requests are refused.

## Ownership and extension

`modules/auth.ts` contains your application's models, credential checking, routes and live-session
resolver. `ket.workspace.ts` connects these to KetJS with an explicit permission resolver. Returning
an empty grant list denies protected operations; `null` would disable that restriction.

The starter opts into `sessions.requireCompany: false`. Adding company-scoped models still requires
real membership resolution; the opt-in does not permit company data without scope. Existing KetSuite
deployments keep the default company-required session policy and their own account models.

The first provisioned account creates a Member role with the sample operations. Later accounts share
that role. Extend the role/grant policy in your module as your application grows. Disabling a user or
changing their `securityVersion` rejects their cookie on the next request. Grants are read live.

For a deployed environment, configure a stable `KET_SECRET` shared by its replicas. Local development
uses the framework's reported ephemeral signing key, so restarting may require signing in again.
Login accepts bounded JSON, checks origin and has a durable five-attempt-per-minute limit per peer.
Configure the network edge and policy for your deployment before exposing it publicly.

## Verify and publish contracts

```bash
# Run from: __NAME__
npm test
npm run openapi
npm run api:docs
```

The integration test provisions an account, logs in through HTTP, writes and reads a note, and signs
out. The OpenAPI reference describes the protected note operations. Use the published framework
version containing this starter; a local candidate build is not a replacement for a release.
